# xv6-riscv

xv6 is a teaching operating system: a compact reimplementation of Dennis Ritchie’s and Ken Thompson’s Unix Version 6, written in ANSI C for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor. It keeps the structure and style of Sixth Edition Unix while using contemporary hardware features—paging, traps, and device interrupts—so the whole kernel can be read and modified in a course.

xv6 is the operating system used in MIT’s 6.1810 (Operating System Engineering). It is meant to be understood, not deployed. Simplifications and clearer code matter more here than new features.

## What is included

The kernel is a small monolithic system that boots on the QEMU `virt` machine:

| Area | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| Processes | Creation, exit, wait, scheduling, and context switch across up to 8 CPUs (3 by default in QEMU) |
| Memory | Physical page allocator and a per-process page table, with a trampoline for user/kernel transitions |
| Traps | System calls, timer interrupts, and device interrupts through the PLIC |
| File system | Buffer cache, logging, inodes, directories, pipes, and a virtio block disk |
| Console | UART console and a small shell |

User programs built into the disk image include `sh`, `ls`, `cat`, `echo`, `grep`, `wc`, `mkdir`, `rm`, `ln`, `kill`, `zombie`, plus stress and regression programs (`usertests`, `grind`, `stressfs`, `logstress`, `forktest`, `forphan`, `dorphan`, `sync`). The shell starts from `init`.

Limits such as the maximum number of processes (`NPROC`), open files, and file-system size live in `kernel/param.h`.

## Repository layout

```
kernel/     Kernel sources (C and RISC-V assembly)
user/       User-space programs and the small C library
mkfs/       Host tool that builds fs.img
Makefile    Kernel, user programs, disk image, and QEMU targets
test-xv6.py Boots xv6 under QEMU and runs named tests
```

## Requirements

Build and run xv6 on Linux, macOS, or Windows Subsystem for Linux. You need:

- GNU Make
- A RISC-V ELF cross compiler. The Makefile looks, in order, for `riscv64-unknown-elf-`, `riscv64-elf-`, `riscv64-none-elf-`, `riscv64-linux-gnu-`, and `riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu-`. A newlib toolchain from the [RISC-V GNU toolchain](https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain) is the usual choice; Debian and Ubuntu’s `gcc-riscv64-linux-gnu` also works.
- `qemu-system-riscv64` version 7.2 or newer
- Perl (to generate `user/usys.S`) and a host C compiler (to build `mkfs`)
- Python 3, if you run `test-xv6.py`
- `bc`, used by the QEMU version check
- `clang-format`, if you run `make fmt`

On Ubuntu or Debian:

```sh
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y build-essential gcc-riscv64-linux-gnu qemu-system-misc python3 perl bc clang-format
```

On macOS, with Homebrew:

```sh
brew install riscv64-elf-gcc qemu python3
```

If the cross compiler is not on `PATH`, set the prefix explicitly:

```sh
make TOOLPREFIX=riscv64-linux-gnu-
```

## Build and run

From the repository root:

```sh
make qemu
```

That builds `kernel/kernel`, builds the user programs, creates `fs.img` with `mkfs`, and starts QEMU with 128 MiB of RAM, three CPUs, no BIOS, a serial console, and a virtio block device backed by `fs.img`. The number of CPUs can be changed up to `NCPU`:

```sh
make CPUS=1 qemu
```

QEMU is started with `-nographic`. The xv6 shell prompt is `$`. Leave QEMU with `Ctrl-A` then `X`.

Other useful targets:

| Command | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `make qemu-gdb` | Boot QEMU paused and wait for GDB. Prints the port to connect to. |
| `make print-gdbport` | Print the GDB TCP port (`25000` plus your user id modulo 5000). |
| `make kernel/kernel` | Build only the kernel. |
| `make fs.img` | Rebuild the file-system image. `README` is copied onto it. |
| `make clean` | Remove objects, the kernel, `fs.img`, and generated sources. |
| `make fmt` | Format kernel, user, and `mkfs` C sources with `clang-format`. |

## Debugging

In one terminal:

```sh
make qemu-gdb
```

In another, from this directory (so `.gdbinit` is picked up):

```sh
riscv64-unknown-elf-gdb kernel/kernel
```

Use the `gdb` binary that matches your toolchain prefix (`riscv64-linux-gnu-gdb`, `riscv64-elf-gdb`, and so on). `.gdbinit` is generated from `.gdbinit.tmpl-riscv` and points GDB at the QEMU stub.

`make qemu` and `make qemu-gdb` also write `kernel/kernel.asm` and `kernel/kernel.sym`, which are the disassembly and symbol table of the linked kernel.

## Testing

`test-xv6.py` boots xv6 and drives the shell without manual input:

```sh
./test-xv6.py usertests
./test-xv6.py -q usertests
./test-xv6.py crash
./test-xv6.py log
```

`-q` runs the shorter subset of `usertests`. `crash` and `log` exercise file-system recovery after a crash. The script rebuilds the kernel and disk image when it needs a clean file system.

Continuous integration on Linux builds the kernel and disk image, checks `clang-format`, and runs `usertests` and the crash tests. The macOS job checks that the kernel and disk image still build.

## Scope

xv6 implements the ideas needed to teach an operating system course: processes, virtual memory, system calls, interrupts, a buffer cache, and a logging file system. It does not aim to be a general-purpose Unix. Device support is the QEMU `virt` UART, PLIC, and virtio disk. There is no networking, and the scheduler, file system, and shell are deliberately small.

Bug reports and suggestions are welcome, especially when they make the code simpler or clearer. Send them to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris at kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu. Course materials and pointers to Sixth Edition Unix sources are at <https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/>.

